hi there steve goffin here today i want  to talk about pronunciation because and  by the way the word is  pronunciation not pronunciation i  sometimes hear people say pronunciation  that's not the word it's pronunciation  but um  i'm doing that for a couple of reasons  first of all i i put out a blog post  where i said that uh  you know we don't need to  pretend or believe that we're gonna  sound like a native essentially we  really really do i've said this before i  can't i'm and yet people the people who  speak really well  are those that don't try to nail down  every last bit of native like  pronunciation you don't you don't have  to however we have to pronounce well  enough that people can understand us and  that it's not unpleasant to listen to it  has to be clear you don't want to be  asking a bigger partner what  what did you say so how do we get to  good pronunciation in the language  well i think there are three elements  one is the attitude  towards pronunciation i'll explain that  the second thing is the most important  things that we can do  that will give us good pronunciation and  the third thing is you know  remedial things if we have what we  consider to be poor pronunciation what  can we do  so  first of all  i think the attitude is overwhelming  in other words the people who pronounce  well have the right attitude  i've said this before  people who are able to get outside of  their own culture  and get into another culture  so i mentioned for example professional  athletes who are part of a team they  want to belong to the team they're going  to end up speaking like the other people  on the team  whereas say an intellectual like a  professor  is less inclined to do that tends to  stay more within his his own culture  we have to get outside of ourselves  that's why for example actors and  actresses are very good at imitating  accents because their whole profession  consists of imitating someone else  another role they can project themselves  into another role  so  you know we have to have that attitude  i've called it cultural weightlessness  i'm not for example me an  english-speaking canadian trying to  learn arabic i want to be with them i  want to be part of their culture or  persian or french or japanese or  whatever the case may be we have to be  able to project ourselves to pretend  that we are this other person and if we  hang back in our own culture  the more we hang back the more we  sort of see ourselves as almost a  representative of that culture  as opposed to just being a person of no  defined  you know  identity learning this new language  the poor we do we have to get outside  that so that's  number one  attitude  number two is  what are the steps that are going to  give us good pronunciation  i think the biggest thing is  listening  when i start a new language and  inevitably in these books they'll have  you know pages devoted to the  pronunciation of the language i spend  very little time on that mostly i want  to understand the value of the letters  so is the letter c  uh so  those are some of the things that i may  not pick up the first time i listen so i  kind of scan through it  some of the letters the vowels the  description of the vowels  it's kind of meaningless until i start  to hear it i have never  bothered to learn the international  phonetic alphabet i don't find it useful  because i know that i'm going to have to  do so much listening  in order to get a sense of the  pronunciation that  it's not really necessary to  to  focus on the ipa and the ipa would  probably represent some standard form of  pronunciation which maybe what i'm  listening to it doesn't correspond to  that anyway so to me that's not a useful  place to spend your time you have to  spend a lot of time listening  there was i someone on my twitter feed  mentioned i think she's an english  teacher in prague  that she had a girl in her class who had  wonderful american-like pronunciation  and the reason was that she has spent a  lot of time watching american television  programs  in the original  and uh you know in sweden everyone  pronounces well and it's because the  kids listen to a lot of tv programs in  the original so i personally don't think  there's any advantage in starting to  speak early and producing the language  early because i think there is a  a danger and i haven't been able to sort  of prove this through any research but i  think there is a danger that obviously  when we haven't heard a lot of the  language it's very difficult to  pronounce it correctly and so we  possibly  ingrain certain pronunciations that are  based on our native language because  that's all we have as a point of  reference  and uh  and so  until we've heard enough of it we start  producing it incorrectly and now we have  to change those pronunciations  you know in order to get closer to  the way the language is actually  pronounced  so  i mean if you had  the opportunity to spend six months  improving your language enough improving  your vocabulary enough that you could  actually enjoyably watch  television program soap operas movies  whatever  listen to podcasts in  the target language  and you didn't speak  and then you started speaking  your pronunciation will be better  than it would have been at the very  beginning before you had heard a lot of  the language  so  i think  a tremendous emphasis should be  placed on massive listening and and  because  why would you if you hear the word  pronounced a certain way  why wouldn't you pronounce it that way  when it's time for you to pronounce it  why would you pronounce it some other  way  in most cases it's not that we are  incapable of producing the sound  it's that we identify we see a word and  for whatever reason it triggers a  certain pronunciation based largely on  our own language yeah there are some  sounds that are difficult to pronounce  in every language there are a few sounds  that are difficult to pronounce and we  never we may never be able to pronounce  them correctly  but most of the sounds we have  and so why aren't we using those sounds  why as i often mentioned my father who  was born in czechoslovakia or was you  know the austro-hungarian empire when he  was born he would say nova scotia  instead of nova scotia  s-c-o-t-i-a it's a province in canada  he heard it all the time that he would  always say nova scotia it's not that he  can't say scotia but for whatever reason  in his mind it's scotia so i think there  are many examples of that where we are  capable of making the sounds but  for whatever reason because we didn't  get enough exposure to the language  before we started speaking we see a word  it triggers that reaction and bear in  mind like especially in english and i'm  finding that in arabic and persian  i have trouble pronouncing a word that i  haven't seen before  so  because the la the value of the letters  can change in persian it's  [Music]  you know they don't have vowels it's  just very difficult to pronounce a word  that i haven't seen before so i end up  relying  on my memory of how the word is  pronounced if i see a word if i see a  new word i'm guessing it how it's  pronounced but hopefully i'll hear it  often enough that i'll be able to  pronounce it but what is driving my  pronunciation is what i hear  and so i think we have to devote a lot  of time to a lot of listening so that  the brain just assimilates it it becomes  natural this is naturally how it's  pronounced now if you do all of this  listening  you're still not going to be like a  native because there will be  sounds that we can't produce like who in  swedish or there's a whole number of  these in different languages that are  difficult to pronounce  um  but and even if we are imitating what we  heard or what we  think we hear it's never going to be a  hundred percent  and  i mean i have dealt with so many  non-native speakers of english who speak  english very very well  but i can't think of any person who  learned the language as an adult who is  genuinely can be mistaken for a native  those people are so rare as to not  really matter  overwhelmingly you're going to develop  with through a lot of listening  good pronunciation good enough easily  understood  now the final thing is if you though  still aren't satisfied with your  pronunciation are there some things you  can do well obviously again listening  and then  reading something listening to it  reading it recording yourself and  comparing comparing not only for the  pronunciation of the individual word but  also  of the tone of the intonation listen to  yourself see  this trains your brain to hear how your  pronunciation is different from the  native and then go back and listen again  and  try to notice these things when you're  doing your  uh you know listening for pleasure  so reading out loud can help  um as i say recording yourself and  comparing yourself to the native speaker  with a particular emphasis on intonation  but  don't spend too much time on this  because this is going to make you  self-conscious  that i go back to the first  condition which is your attitude if you  are relaxed if you are comfortable  if you enjoy speaking the language if  you can picture yourself as just being  part of this other group where you're  just communicating for the purpose of  communicating if you do that without a  sense of pressure you will pronounce  well enough you won't be mistaken for a  native but you will achieve what i  always strive to achieve in any language  and that is to be comfortably understood  by the people i'm speaking to anyway  that's what i have to say on  pronunciation bye for now
 

